// 2026-05-12 hardening: behind Vercel / Nginx / any reverse proxy,
// Express by default returns the socket peer IP (always the proxy's
// loopback) for `req.ip` — so rate limiters that hash on `req.ip` end
// up bucketing ALL traffic into one key. Two failure modes:
//   1. Legit users get throttled by attackers (one bad actor fills
//      the global bucket and everyone else gets 429).
//   2. The 10 req/min limit on Anthropic-burning endpoints becomes a
//      CLUSTER-wide ceiling, making the service unusable for everyone
//      while the attacker pays nothing.
// `trust proxy: 1` tells Express to honor X-Forwarded-For from EXACTLY
// one upstream hop (our Vercel/Nginx terminator). Combined with
// Sunny's x-vercel-forwarded-for fix in _lib/ratelimit.js (the Vercel
// serverless equivalent), rate limiting now keys on real client IP.
// NOTE: setting `true` here would trust ANY X-Forwarded-For value,
// re-introducing the very XFF-spoof bypass Sunny just patched. Always 1.
app.set('trust proxy', 1);

